.lua
book.chapter(15, "Algebraic data and domain models")
.endlua
.sh 2 "A sum of named alternatives"
.pp
An algebraic data type declares a finite family of constructors. Each
constructor names a case and carries its own argument types. This expresses
alternatives directly: a shape can be a circle with one radius or a rectangle
with two dimensions. A record with optional radius, width, and height would
also admit meaningless combinations. Constructors let the representation rule
out those combinations before the interpreter of the model is written.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[type Shape := | Circle Float | Rectangle Float Float
area shape := match shape with
  | Circle r -> 3.141592653589793 * r * r
  | Rectangle w h -> w * h
Test.equal "rectangle payload" (area (Rectangle 3.0 4.0)) 12.0
Test.equal "circle boundary" (area (Circle 0.0)) 0.0]=], "Constructors encode alternative shapes")
.endlua
.pp
Use a leading vertical bar to make an ADT declaration unambiguous, especially
when one constructor might otherwise resemble a type alias. Constructor names
begin uppercase. Until enough arguments are supplied, a constructor behaves
as a curried function. Once saturated, it produces a value carrying its tag
and payloads. Patterns recover those payloads without exposing mutable
representation fields or requiring a string comparison against a tag name.
.sh 2 "Parameterized types express reusable structure"
.pp
A type parameter abstracts over the payload while retaining the container's
shape. Box 'a can contain any one chosen type, and a function unboxing it
preserves that relationship. Option and Result are built-in examples: Option
distinguishes absence from a present value, and Result distinguishes success
from a payload-bearing error. Their constructors exist in a fresh session;
the convenience functions arrive with the portable library.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[type Box 'a := | Box 'a
unbox (Box value) := value
Test.equal "generic payload" (unbox (Box "message")) "message"
Test.equal "different instantiation" (unbox (Box 42)) 42
type Validation := | Accepted Int | Rejected String
validate n := if n >= 0 then Accepted n else Rejected "negative count"
Test.equal "explicit state" (validate (~1)) (Rejected "negative count")]=],
    "Reusable containers and domain-specific outcomes")
.endlua
.pp
An ADT is a stronger interface than a collection of magic strings. A misspelled
constructor is a name error, while a misspelled status string might travel
through several layers before a consumer notices it. Payload types also make
illegal combinations harder to express. Accepted carries the validated
integer, so a consumer does not need to look elsewhere for a possibly unrelated
number after testing a status flag.
.sh 2 "Recursive data models recursive syntax"
.pp
Expression trees are a natural use of recursive ADTs. A literal contains an
integer; an addition contains two expressions; a multiplication contains two
expressions. The recursive type describes finite syntax trees, and a recursive
interpreter follows the same structure. This is the bridge from ordinary
functional programming to meta-programming: the program's input is another
program represented as a typed value.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[type Expr := | Lit Int | Add Expr Expr | Mul Expr Expr
evaluate expr := match expr with
  | Lit n -> n
  | Add a b -> evaluate a + evaluate b
  | Mul a b -> evaluate a * evaluate b
tree := Add (Lit 2) (Mul (Lit 3) (Lit 4))
Test.equal "tree interpretation" (evaluate tree) 14]=], "An expression language as an algebraic data type")
book.graph({
    {id="A", label="Add", x=0, y=0},
    {id="L", label="Lit 2", x=-1.3, y=-1.1},
    {id="M", label="Mul", x=1.3, y=-1.1},
    {id="X", label="Lit 3", x=0.2, y=-2.2},
    {id="Y", label="Lit 4", x=2.4, y=-2.2}
}, {{"A", "L"}, {"A", "M"}, {"M", "X"}, {"M", "Y"}},
    "The tree preserves precedence structurally")
.endlua
.pp
Once the tree exists, precedence is no longer an ambiguity: multiplication is
the right child of addition. A parser's job includes constructing this shape
from text. A pretty-printer's job includes emitting enough parentheses to
preserve the shape's meaning. An optimizer's job includes changing the shape
without changing the specified semantics. Giving each stage a clear input
and output representation makes those responsibilities testable independently.
.sh 2 "Interpretations share structure, not purpose"
.pp
The same Expr can support evaluation, rendering, free-variable analysis,
constant folding, or code generation. Each interpretation visits the
constructors according to its own result type. A renderer returns String; an
evaluator returns Int or Result; an analyzer may return a set-like list of
names. This reuse is one reason algebraic data models are effective for
language tools. The syntax does not need to be reparsed for every analysis.
.pp
A generic Ast is more flexible about tags, but weaker about domain invariants.
An ADT restricts the possible cases statically, while a generic node can have
an arbitrary tag and number of children. A practical architecture parses
into a generic tree, validates and lowers it into a domain ADT, then performs
semantic passes on the stronger representation. Chapter 31 follows exactly
that pattern rather than letting unchecked child indexing spread throughout
the compiler.
.sh 2 "Representation is not an interchange format"
.pp
Constructor values have structural equality and ordering where their payloads
support it. Incidental constructor ordering should not become a durable
business ranking or file-format rule. Core.show is convenient inspection
notation, but adding a constructor or renaming a type should not silently
change a public protocol. Write explicit encoders and decoders when values
cross process, storage, or version boundaries.
.pp
Signatures can hide an implementation type while exposing operations that
construct and inspect it. This lets a module retain the benefits of an ADT
internally without promising every constructor to clients. Hiding a
representation is especially valuable when invariants extend beyond shape,
such as a nonnegative count or a normalized expression. A constructor's type
alone cannot establish every semantic property of its payload.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read datatype declarations and constructor typing in the runtime, then inspect
the built-in Option and Result definitions and ASTBuilder's Ast declaration.
Extend the expression model with subtraction and add both evaluation and
rendering cases. Test that nested subtraction prints with enough parentheses.
Then add a validation boundary for a domain-specific constructor whose payload
must be positive. Explain which invariant is guaranteed by the ADT's shape
and which must be checked by the constructor function.
